-names>TheresaC</given-names></name><name><surname>Amish</surname><given-names>Shah</given-names></name><name><surname>Jessica</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Tierney</surname><given-names/></name><name><surname>Tomm</surname><given-names>LouiseL</given-names></name><name><surname>O'Gara</surname><given-names>ThomasM</given-names></name><name><surname>Goranov</surname><given-names>AI</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Genetic Composition of the <italic>Bacillus subtilis </italic>SOS System</article-title><source>J Bacteriol</source><year>2005</year><volume>187</volume><fpage>7655</fpage><lpage>7666</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16267290</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1128/JB.187.22.7655-7666.2005</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B66"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>The sequencing is performed using 454 technologies. Preliminary results using TIGR annotation of the contigs identified by sequencing indicate that the genome comprised about 6,800 open reading frames (ORFs). The results also indicate the existence of surfactin synthesis related genes as well as the existence of 42 flagella-related genes and several key chemotaxis-related genes including CheA, CheC, CheD and CheW, which are organized in 6 operons. In regard to the SOS response we have identified homology with several key SOS genes such as RecA, LexA, DinB, UvrA and Uvr B. The sequencing effort is conducted under the Tauber Initiative at Tel Aviv University in collaboration with GeneBee group at Moscow State University, The Genome center at the Weizmann Institute of Science and DYN-GS Israel, E. Ben Jacob (unpublished)</article-title></citation></ref><ref id="B67"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Arnold</surname><given-names>JW</given-names></name><name><surname>Shimkets</surname><given-names>LJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Inhibition of cell-cell interactions in <italic>Myxococcus xanthus </italic>by Congo Red</article-title><source>J Bacteriol</source><year>1988</year><volume>170</volume><fpage>5765</fpage><lpage>5770</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3142856</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B68"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kinsinger</surname><given-names>RF</given-names></name><name><surname>Kearns</surname><given-names>DB</given-names></name><name><surname>Hale</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Fall</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Genetic requirements for potassium ion-dependent colony spreading in <italic>Bacillus subtilis</italic></article-title><source>J Bacteriol</source><year>2005</year><volume>187</volume><fpage>8462</fpage><lpage>8469</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16321950</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1128/JB.187.24.8462-8469.2005</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B69"><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jha</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Slime mould used to create first robot run by living cells</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.guardian.co.uk/science/story/0,,1709944,00.html"/></citation></ref><ref id="B70"><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rasband</surname><given-names>WS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>ImageJ</article-title><source>National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, Maryland, USA</source><year>2004</year><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://rsb.info.nih.gov/ij/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B71"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ingham</surname><given-names>CJ</given-names></name><name><surname>van den Ende</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Wever</surname><given-names>PC</given